---
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PhoneVideo from '../../components/PhoneVideo.astro';
import RouteFlow from '../../components/RouteFlow.astro';

// Drop a screen recording at public/tourasna-demo.mp4 (and optionally public/tourasna-demo.jpg as the poster)
// and the video block appears on the next build.
const hasVideo = existsSync(join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'tourasna-demo.mp4'));
const hasLens = existsSync(join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'tourasna-lens.mp4'));
const hasPoster = existsSync(join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'tourasna-demo.jpg'));

const numbers = [
  ['Detector', 'YOLOv8m, 767 Gardiner sign classes', 'mAP@50 0.019 on validation (best epoch 40 of 50)'],
  ['Translator', 'Transformer seq2seq, 11.8M parameters, from scratch', 'BLEU 7.76 on validation'],
  ['Reading order', 'Rule-based quadrat sorter', 'Direction chosen by the user (automatic detection reached about 62%, so I dropped it)'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Tourasna hieroglyph translator | Moaz Elkhashab"
  description="A four-layer photo-to-translation pipeline for Egyptian hieroglyphs: YOLOv8 detection, reading-order sorting, and a fallback chain that always returns something honest. Part of the Tourasna graduation project."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head grid">
      <!-- TODO(moaz): rewrite the title and lead in your own voice. -->
      <h1>Tourasna: a hieroglyph translator that says when it does not know</h1>
      <p class="lead col-text">
        A tourist uploads a photo of an inscription. My service detects the signs, puts them in reading order, and
        returns an English and Arabic translation with the layer that produced it. It is one feature of Tourasna, our
        graduation project, where I was team leader of 6. I built this whole service, and below it, the other parts of
        the app I built or started.
      </p>
      <ul class="links col-wide">
        <li><a href="https://github.com/Tourasna/Tourasna">Code (the translator is in ai-service/hieroglyphics-api)</a></li>
      </ul>
    </header>

    {hasVideo && (
      <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="vid">
        <div class="col-wide">
          <h2 id="vid">The app, running</h2>
          <PhoneVideo
            src="/tourasna-demo.mp4"
            poster={hasPoster ? '/tourasna-demo.jpg' : ''}
            ratio="486 / 990"
            label="Screen recording of the Tourasna app translating a photographed inscription."
            caption="Screen recording from the Tourasna app."
          />
        </div>
      </section>
    )}

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="pipe">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="pipe">The pipeline</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>1. Detection.</strong> YOLOv8m, fine-tuned on a Kaggle T4, finds each sign and its Gardiner code,
          keeping detections above 0.40 confidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>2. Reading order.</strong> A rule-based algorithm groups overlapping glyphs into quadrats, the
          vertical stacks that hieroglyphic text uses, and sorts them right-to-left, left-to-right or top-to-bottom.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>3. Translation, four layers, first hit wins.</strong> A hand-curated table of 32 phrases (royal
          names and common formulas). Then an LLM (Llama 3.3 70B through Groq) that returns a confidence and is told to
          answer “unknown” rather than guess. Then my own transformer, which needs no internet. Last, the meaning of
          each single sign, so the service always returns something.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="col-note">The response also carries a transliteration, historical context, and which layer answered.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="num">
      <div class="col-wide">
        <h2 id="num">The numbers, as they are</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {numbers.map(([a, b, c]) => <tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="meta">
          The detector's mAP is low. With 767 visually close classes, a right glyph family with the wrong variant counts
          as a miss, and on tourist photos the mean confidence is 0.63 to 0.69. Confidence is not accuracy, and I did not
          measure end-to-end accuracy on photos.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="data">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="data">Data, and the bug I found in it</h2>
        <p>
          The detector trains on 32,362 images with 537,682 annotations (27,937 train, 3,460 validation, 965 test). In
          that dataset 48,549 annotation lines had class IDs above 766, outside the 767 classes. I removed only those
          lines and kept every valid one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The translator trains on the BBAW Egyptian corpus: 35,497 usable rows, 34,278 clean pairs after filtering
          (29,136 train, 3,427 validation, 1,715 test). The corpus is mostly German, so I machine-translated it to
          English with Google Translate. The English targets are therefore machine output, and that caps what the model
          can learn.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also trained a sign classifier (EfficientNetB0, 98.4% test accuracy on 117 classes) and removed it from the
          pipeline, because the detector already returns classes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="eng">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="eng">Service and honesty by design</h2>
        <p>
          A FastAPI service with 7 endpoints and 40 integration tests. A correction endpoint lets the user fix detected
          codes before translating, which is the answer to blurry or eroded inscriptions. Given invented codes, the LLM
          layer returns “unknown” with low confidence instead of a made-up translation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
        <p>
          The reading direction is chosen by the user. The LLM layer needs internet, while the transformer and the
          per-sign layer work offline. The curated table has only 32 phrases, so most real inscriptions rely on the
          LLM. There is no hosted demo, because the detector needs a GPU server and deployment belonged to the backend
          team.
        </p>
        <p>
          The team built the app around these features: the Flutter shell, the NestJS backend, the AWS deployment, the
          recommender and the FAHMY chatbot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="mine">
      <div class="col-wide">
        <h2 id="mine">Also in Tourasna: what else I built</h2>
        <ul class="mine">
          <li>
            <p class="tag">Built by me</p>
            <h3>Interactive map</h3>
            <p>
              Live routing, numbered stops, trip history and a calendar, and voice guidance that starts by itself when
              you reach a place. Eight steps from an agenda to a story told aloud, below.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="tag">Started by me, taken further with a teammate</p>
            <h3>AI Lens</h3>
            <p>
              I built the first version at small scale and showed that recognising a monument from a photo worked. When
              it needed to cover 140+ Cairo landmarks, a teammate took over the modelling, and I built what it needed
              to get there: I collected the extra images and annotated them.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="tag">Pipeline by me, models made with a teammate</p>
            <h3>3D viewer</h3>
            <p>
              I designed the pipeline: a 3D model from photos (TripoSR, Hyper3D), stored as GLB on Google Cloud Storage
              and shown in the app with drag to rotate and magnification. Once it worked, a teammate produced the rest of
              the models, and the library reached 30+ landmarks.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="map">
      <div class="col-wide mapgrid">
        <div class="shot">
          <div class="device"><img src="/img/tourasna-map.jpg" width="738" height="1600" alt="The Tourasna interactive map showing day 1 of a plan: three numbered stops joined by a route, 23.7 km and 43 minutes, with buttons to open the day plan and start the trip." loading="lazy" /></div>
          <p class="vcap">Day 1 of a plan: three stops, 23.7 km, 43 min.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 id="map">The interactive map</h2>
          <p class="mp">
            I built it. A traveller picks a start, the app routes the day, and when they are
            within 100 m of a stop it tells that place’s story aloud. Trips can be paused, resumed or skipped, and they
            are saved to a history and the calendar. Data lives in Firebase with a local JSON fallback.
          </p>
          <RouteFlow />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {hasLens && (
      <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="lens">
        <div class="col-wide">
          <h2 id="lens">AI Lens to 3D viewer, in the app</h2>
          <PhoneVideo
            src="/tourasna-lens.mp4"
            poster="/tourasna-lens.jpg"
            label="Screen recording: the AI Lens identifies the Mask of Tutankhamun and the Muhammad Ali Mosque, and the 3D viewer shows the mask."
            chapters={[
              { t: 0, label: 'Open the AI Lens', note: 'From the home screen of the app.' },
              { t: 2, label: 'Point at a monument', note: 'Take a photo of an artefact or a building.' },
              { t: 5, label: 'Identified', note: 'The Mask of Tutankhamun, with its story and a 3D badge.' },
              { t: 8, label: 'The 3D viewer', note: 'The model streams in from the cloud, then you rotate and magnify it.' },
              { t: 24, label: 'Another monument', note: 'The Muhammad Ali Mosque, recognised the same way.' },
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </section>
    )}

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: 40px;
    row-gap: 24px;
  }
  .head h1 {
    grid-column: 1 / span 10;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6.5vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .mine { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .mine li { background: var(--paper); padding: 22px 22px 26px; transition: background 0.2s; }
  .mine li:hover { background: var(--panel); }
  .mine h3 { margin: 4px 0 8px; font-size: 24px; }
  .mine p { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.55; }
  .mine .tag { color: var(--teal); font-size: 12.5px; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0; }
  .mapgrid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 250px) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 48px; align-items: start; }
  .shot .device { padding: 10px; border-radius: 38px; background: #1a1d1f; border: 1px solid #3a4144; box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7); overflow: hidden; }
  .shot img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 28px; }
  .mp { color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 24px; max-width: 60ch; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .mapgrid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; justify-items: start; } .shot { width: min(260px, 70vw); } }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 15px; }
  th, td { text-align: start; padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 600; }
  .vcap { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 14px; margin-top: 10px; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .head h1 { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  }
</style>
